WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:02.509
(lively, bouncy music)

00:00:02.509 --> 00:00:05.469
- When I realized that monuments of those

00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:07.507
who served our country and
fought for the freedoms

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.910
that we enjoy today were in disrepair

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:13.398
and poor condition, uh, it upset me.

00:00:13.398 --> 00:00:17.180
It's sad to see that they've
been forgotten, completely.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.308
Most of them are unreadable,
so no one can tell

00:00:19.308 --> 00:00:22.127
who they are, what they
did, and how they served

00:00:22.127 --> 00:00:25.037
our country, and what
they meant to our country.

00:00:25.037 --> 00:00:27.527
If you find a gleaming
stone that may be 100

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.866
or 150 years old, there's a good chance

00:00:29.866 --> 00:00:32.366
that I've probably been there.

00:00:35.103 --> 00:00:36.997
My name is Andrew Lumish, or better known

00:00:36.997 --> 00:00:39.506
as The Good Cemeterian.

00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.064
I restore the monuments
of veterans who served

00:00:42.064 --> 00:00:46.101
in multiple conflicts for our
country and their families.

00:00:46.101 --> 00:00:48.533
I complete restorations
on my day off typically,

00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:49.792
which is Sunday.

00:00:49.792 --> 00:00:54.591
I've restored between five
and 600 monuments to date.

00:00:54.591 --> 00:00:56.896
Living in the South, there
is a multitude of things

00:00:56.896 --> 00:00:59.275
that accumulate on a given stone.

00:00:59.275 --> 00:01:01.625
It may have more than 100 years

00:01:01.625 --> 00:01:05.505
of buildup and mildew and
mold, and it's very tedious,

00:01:05.505 --> 00:01:08.636
it's very hard work,
soaking a stone with water,

00:01:08.636 --> 00:01:09.924
scrubbing with brushes.

00:01:09.924 --> 00:01:13.135
There are toothbrushes and Q-tips.

00:01:13.135 --> 00:01:15.905
A monument restoration
typically will take between

00:01:15.905 --> 00:01:18.749
two and four months, but there's nothing

00:01:18.749 --> 00:01:23.704
that makes me feel as good as
when I complete a restoration.

00:01:23.704 --> 00:01:26.855
The reason I began doing
this is for both personal

00:01:26.855 --> 00:01:28.784
and historical reasons.

00:01:28.784 --> 00:01:32.060
I have friends who serve
and friends who I lost

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:36.191
both in battle in combat, and
friends who lost their battle

00:01:36.191 --> 00:01:38.036
once they came back home.

00:01:38.036 --> 00:01:41.794
It's important to me to see
that they can be remembered

00:01:41.794 --> 00:01:43.294
and not forgotten.

00:01:44.368 --> 00:01:48.149
The monuments themselves
are not just stones.

00:01:48.149 --> 00:01:51.218
I want to uncover who they
were as a human being,

00:01:51.218 --> 00:01:54.164
as a person, in addition
to their sacrifice

00:01:54.164 --> 00:01:56.077
and their service to our country,

00:01:56.077 --> 00:01:58.149
because they weren't just soldiers either,

00:01:58.149 --> 00:01:59.699
they were Americans first.

00:01:59.699 --> 00:02:01.287
The were soldiers second.

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.204
I can't allow that to be forgotten.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:11.847
(chiming)

